\fs96 \cf2 GPU block and warp operations and synchronization\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa400\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 When multiple GPU threads write to the same memory location without a defined order of execution, a race condition occurs. The final outcome of the computation becomes non-deterministic, depending on the scheduling and timing of execution of threads by the GPU hardware. Such bugs are notoriously difficult to debug because they may not appear consistently in every run.\
To write correct and robust parallel programs, you need explicit mechanisms to coordinate the execution of threads and manage the visibility of their memory operations. These mechanisms are known as synchronization primitives. They are not merely performance optimizations; they are essential tools for correctness. Without them, threads operate in complete isolation, unable to safely share intermediate results, divide complex tasks, or perform the collective computations that are the hallmark of high-performance GPU algorithms.\
A barrier is a fundamental synchronization primitive that creates a meeting point in the program where all participating threads must wait for each other. When a thread reaches a barrier, it pauses execution until every other thread in the group also arrives. This ensures that all threads proceed together past the barrier, maintaining consistent state and preventing race conditions when accessing shared data.\
Mojo provides two complementary categories of GPU coordination tools. Synchronization primitives like 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 barrier()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  and 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 syncwarp()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  coordinate thread execution and enforce memory visibility, but they don't perform computation themselves\'97they're pure coordination mechanisms. In contrast, collective operations like reductions, broadcasts, and prefix sums combine synchronization with common computational patterns: they coordinate threads and compute a result. Use synchronization primitives when you need explicit control over when threads coordinate (such as managing access to shared memory between distinct phases of an algorithm), and use collective operations when you need to aggregate or distribute data across threads (such as computing a sum or maximum across a thread block or warp). Both types of tools are essential for writing correct and efficient GPU code, and understanding when to use each is key to building robust parallel algorithms.\
This guide covers Mojo's low-level primitives for managing coordination at the thread block and warp levels. For foundational GPU architecture concepts and detailed explanations of the GPU execution model, see {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/manual/gpu/architecture/"}}{\fldrslt \cf5 \ul \ulc5 Intro to GPUs}}. For a discussion of basic kernel creation and device management, see {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/manual/gpu/fundamentals/"}}{\fldrslt \cf5 \ul \ulc5 GPU programming fundamentals}}.\
We'll explore Mojo's synchronization and collective communication primitives for coordinating parallel work on the GPU. Key topics include:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls4\ilvl0\cf5 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\ul Block-level synchronization and operations\cf2 \ulnone : How to coordinate all threads within a thread block using {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/std/gpu/sync/sync/barrier/"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 \ul \ulc5 barrier()}} and block reduction operations from the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/std/gpu/primitives/block/"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 \ul \ulc5 gpu.primitives.block}} module.\
\ls4\ilvl0\cf5 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\ul Warp-level operations\cf2 \ulnone : How to perform fine-grained synchronization with {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/std/gpu/sync/sync/syncwarp/"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 \ul \ulc5 syncwarp()}} and leverage high-speed data exchange using {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/std/gpu/primitives/warp/"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 \ul \ulc5 gpu.primitives.warp}} primitives.\
\ls4\ilvl0\cf5 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\ul Best practices and common pitfalls\cf2 \ulnone : How to use these primitives correctly to write reliable and portable GPU code.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa800\partightenfactor0

\fs64 \cf2 Block-level synchronization and operations\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 This section covers coordination mechanisms for all threads within a thread block:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls5\ilvl0\cf5 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\ul The 
\fs28 \cb12 barrier()
\fs32 \cb1  primitive\cf2 \ulnone : The fundamental synchronization primitive that ensures all threads reach the same point before proceeding.\
\ls5\ilvl0\cf5 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\ul Block-level reduction operations\cf2 \ulnone : Higher-level collective operations (
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 sum
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 max
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 min
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 broadcast
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 prefix_sum
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 ) that combine synchronization with computation.\
\ls5\ilvl0\cf5 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\ul Block synchronization example\cf2 \ulnone : A complete tiled matrix multiplication demonstrating practical barrier usage.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 These tools serve different but complementary purposes: 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 barrier()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  is a pure synchronization primitive for coordinating execution and memory visibility, while block reduction operations are collective computations that internally handle their own synchronization. You can use 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 barrier()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  to build custom coordination patterns, or use block reductions when you need both coordination and computation together.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs48 \cf2 The 
\fs42 \cf10 \cb12 barrier()
\fs48 \cf2 \cb1  primitive\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 The {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/std/gpu/sync/sync/barrier/"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 \ul \ulc5 gpu.sync.barrier()}} function is the primary mechanism for coordinating all threads within a single thread block. It creates a synchronization point in the kernel's execution flow that no thread can pass until every other thread in its block has also reached that point.\
The 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 barrier()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  primitive does two things: it acts as both an execution barrier and a memory fence.\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa160\partightenfactor0
\ls6\ilvl0\cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Execution barrier: As an execution barrier, 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 barrier()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  ensures that the execution of all threads in a block is paused at that point in the program. The hardware scheduler will not allow any thread to proceed past the barrier until all threads in that block have signaled their arrival.\
\ls6\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Memory fence: As a memory fence, 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 barrier()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  enforces a strict ordering on memory operations. It guarantees that all writes to shared memory (and global memory, with respect to other threads in the same block) performed by any thread before the barrier are completed and made visible to all other threads in the block after they pass the barrier. This guarantee is what prevents race conditions when threads communicate via shared memory.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 The most common use case for 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 barrier()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  is managing access to the fast, on-chip shared memory shared by all threads within a block. Here's how a typical algorithm works:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa160\partightenfactor0
\ls7\ilvl0\cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	1	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Threads in a block cooperatively load a segment of data from the high-latency global memory into a shared memory array. Each thread is responsible for loading one or more elements.\
\ls7\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	2	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
A call to 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 barrier()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  is made. This is essential to ensure that the entire data segment is fully loaded into shared memory before any thread attempts to use it.\
\ls7\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	3	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Threads perform computations, reading from and writing to the shared memory array. This phase leverages the low latency of shared memory to accelerate the algorithm.\
\ls7\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	4	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
If the computation itself involves multiple stages of shared memory communication, another 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 barrier()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  call may be necessary to ensure the results of one stage are visible before the next begins.\
\ls7\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	5	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Finally, threads write their results from shared memory back to global memory.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf13 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf13 \cb14 Caution\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \cb14 A 
\fs24\fsmilli12250 \cf10 \cb15 barrier()
\fs28 \cf2 \cb14  must be encountered by all threads within a block to avoid a deadlock. Placing a 
\fs24\fsmilli12250 \cf10 \cb15 barrier()
\fs28 \cf2 \cb14  inside a conditional statement (such as an 
\fs24\fsmilli12250 \cf10 \cb15 if
\fs28 \cf2 \cb14  or 
\fs24\fsmilli12250 \cf10 \cb15 else
\fs28 \cf2 \cb14  block) is a common source of bugs. If the condition causes some threads to execute the 
\fs24\fsmilli12250 \cf10 \cb15 barrier()
\fs28 \cf2 \cb14  while others skip it, the threads that reach the barrier will wait indefinitely for the other threads to arrive, causing the kernel to hang. Therefore, 
\fs24\fsmilli12250 \cf10 \cb15 barrier()
\fs28 \cf2 \cb14  should be used in conditional code only if it is guaranteed that all threads in the block will evaluate the condition identically and follow the same execution path.\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 The Mojo 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 barrier()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  function is functionally equivalent to the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 __syncthreads()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  intrinsic in both NVIDIA CUDA and AMD HIP and 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 threadgroup_barrier(mem_flags::mem_threadgroup)
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  in Apple Metal, providing a portable syntax for this fundamental operation.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf5 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf2 \cb16 For fine-grained synchronization within a single warp, see 
\fs24\fsmilli12250 \cb15 \ul syncwarp()
\fs28 \cb16 \ulnone , which provides faster coordination for threads executing together in the same warp without requiring block-wide synchronization.\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs48 \cf2 Block-level reduction operations\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 In addition to the basic 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 barrier()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  primitive, Mojo provides higher-level block-wide collective operations through the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/std/gpu/primitives/block/"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 \ul \ulc5 gpu.primitives.block}} module. These operations combine the coordination functionality of 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 barrier()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  with common computational patterns, offering both convenience and performance benefits.\
The 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 gpu.primitives.block
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  module includes several reduction primitives:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls8\ilvl0
\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/std/gpu/primitives/block/sum/"}}{\fldrslt \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\ul sum(val)}}
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
: Computes the sum of 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 val
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  across all threads in the block.\
\ls8\ilvl0
\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/std/gpu/primitives/block/max/"}}{\fldrslt \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\ul max(val)}}
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
: Computes the maximum 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 val
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  across all threads in the block.\
\ls8\ilvl0
\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/std/gpu/primitives/block/min/"}}{\fldrslt \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\ul min(val)}}
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
: Computes the minimum 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 val
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  across all threads in the block.\
\ls8\ilvl0
\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/std/gpu/primitives/block/broadcast/"}}{\fldrslt \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\ul broadcast(val, src_thread=0)}}
