\fs28 \cf5 \cb16 Tip\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \cb16 Use 
\fs24\fsmilli12250 \cf10 \cb15 gpu.primitives.block
\fs28 \cf2 \cb16  operations when you need to aggregate data across all threads in a thread block (which may span multiple warps). Use 
\fs24\fsmilli12250 \cf10 \cb15 gpu.primitives.warp
\fs28 \cf2 \cb16  operations, as described in \ul Warp-level reduction operations\ulnone , when you need to aggregate only within a single warp, as they are significantly faster. For algorithms that reduce large datasets, use a hybrid approach: first reduce within warps using 
\fs24\fsmilli12250 \cf10 \cb15 gpu.primitives.warp
\fs28 \cf2 \cb16  primitives, then combine warp results using 
\fs24\fsmilli12250 \cf10 \cb15 gpu.primitives.block
\fs28 \cf2 \cb16  operations.\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs48 \cf2 Using block operations in practice\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 Block-level operations are commonly used in multi-stage algorithms where threads must coordinate through shared memory. A typical pattern involves:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls9\ilvl0\cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	1	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Load phase: Threads cooperatively load data into shared memory\
\ls9\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	2	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Synchronize: Use 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 barrier()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  to ensure all data is loaded\
\ls9\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	3	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Compute phase: Process data using shared memory\
\ls9\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	4	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Reduce phase: Use block reduction operations to aggregate results\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 This pattern appears in algorithms like tiled matrix multiplication, stencil operations, and parallel reductions, where the combination of shared memory and proper synchronization enables significant performance improvements over naive approaches.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs48 \cf2 Block synchronization example: tiled matrix multiplication\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 Matrix multiplication benefits from a technique called tiling, where we break large matrices into smaller tiles that fit in the GPU's fast shared memory. Instead of repeatedly reading from slow global memory, threads in a block cooperatively load a tile into shared memory once, then all threads can access it multiple times. This creates a classic producer-consumer pattern: threads work together to load data (producer phase), then all threads use that data to compute results (consumer phase). Without proper synchronization between these phases, the algorithm produces incorrect results. For a deeper understanding of the tiling strategy, see {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.modular.com/blog/matrix-multiplication-on-nvidias-blackwell-part-2-using-hardware-features-to-optimize-matmul#shared-memory"}}{\fldrslt \cf5 \ul \ulc5 this section of our blog post on optimizing matrix multiplication on NVIDIA's Blackwell}}.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28\fsmilli14400 \cf17 \cb18 tiled_matmul.mojo\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

