\fs48 \cf2 Warp-level synchronization\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 The {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/std/gpu/sync/sync/syncwarp/"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 \ul \ulc5 gpu.sync.syncwarp()}} function provides a more granular synchronization barrier that operates only on the threads within a single warp.\
This function handles thread divergence. On some GPU architectures, threads within a warp can follow different execution paths due to conditional branching. 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 syncwarp()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  forces the specified threads in the warp to reconverge at a single point before proceeding.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf5 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf2 \cb16 For coordinating threads across multiple warps within a thread block, use 
\fs24\fsmilli12250 \cb15 \ul barrier()
\fs28 \cb16 \ulnone  instead, which synchronizes all threads in the block and provides memory fence guarantees for shared memory access.\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 The 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 syncwarp()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  function takes an optional 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 mask
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  argument. This is a 32-bit or 64-bit integer (depending on the warp size of the architecture) that acts as a bitmask. The 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 i
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 th bit of the mask corresponds to the thread at lane 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 i
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  within the warp. If a bit is set to 1, the corresponding thread participates in the synchronization; if it is 0, it does not. The default value of -1 (all bits set to 1) synchronizes all threads in the warp.\
Understanding 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 syncwarp()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  requires knowing its platform-dependent behavior, which Mojo's portable API abstracts away:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa160\partightenfactor0
\ls11\ilvl0\cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
On NVIDIA GPUs supporting independent thread scheduling (Volta architecture and newer), threads within a warp can genuinely diverge. In this context, 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 syncwarp()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  compiles to an active hardware instruction (
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 bar.warp.sync
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 ) that forces the participating threads to wait for each other. It is necessary for correctness in algorithms that rely on warp-synchronous behavior.\
\ls11\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
On AMD GPUs, threads within a wavefront (the AMD equivalent of a warp) are guaranteed by the hardware to execute in lock-step. They cannot diverge in the same way. Consequently, 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 syncwarp()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  is a no-op on AMD architectures; the Mojo compiler emits no instruction for it.\
\ls11\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
On Apple silicon GPUs, this provides only execution synchronization within a SIMD group (the Apple equivalent of a warp), with no memory fence (the Apple Metal equivalent of 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 simdgroup_barrier(mem_flags::mem_none)
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 ). Lane masks are not supported, so the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 mask
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  argument is ignored and all active lanes must reach this point.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 This difference highlights a key benefit of Mojo. You write code against a single, portable API. The compiler is responsible for generating the correct, architecture-specific code. Therefore, if an algorithm relies on 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 syncwarp()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  for correctness on NVIDIA hardware, it still behaves as expected on other vendors' hardware.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf5 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf2 \cb16 \ul \ulc2 Warp shuffle operations\ulnone  (like 
\fs24\fsmilli12250 \cf10 \cb15 shuffle_down()
\fs28 \cf2 \cb16 , 
\fs24\fsmilli12250 \cf10 \cb15 shuffle_xor()
\fs28 \cf2 \cb16 , etc.) and \ul warp reduction operations\ulnone  (like 
\fs24\fsmilli12250 \cf10 \cb15 max()
\fs28 \cf2 \cb16 , 
\fs24\fsmilli12250 \cf10 \cb15 prefix_sum()
\fs28 \cf2 \cb16 , 
\fs24\fsmilli12250 \cf10 \cb15 sum()
\fs28 \cf2 \cb16 , etc.) provide implicit synchronization and do not require 
\fs24\fsmilli12250 \cf10 \cb15 syncwarp()
\fs28 \cf2 \cb16  calls before them. Calling 
\fs24\fsmilli12250 \cf10 \cb15 syncwarp()
\fs28 \cf2 \cb16  before a warp shuffle or reduction operation is redundant and unnecessary.\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs48 \cf2 Warp-level data exchange\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 Shuffle operations are the cornerstone of high-performance warp-level algorithms. These primitives enable threads within a warp to exchange data directly through registers, making them essential for implementing efficient parallel patterns like reductions, stencil computations, and sliding window operations.\
Unlike shared memory communication that requires explicit synchronization and memory transactions, shuffle operations use the warp's simultaneous execution to achieve near-zero latency data exchange. This makes them ideal for:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls12\ilvl0\cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Neighbor data access: Access elements from adjacent threads in stencil operations or convolutions.\
\ls12\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Tree-structured reductions: Implement butterfly patterns for parallel reductions and prefix operations.\
\ls12\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Data broadcasting: Distribute computed values or constants across all threads in a warp.\
\ls12\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Sliding window algorithms: Efficiently compute running maximums, minimums, or moving averages.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

