\cf2 All of these primitives other than 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 broadcast()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  take an optional 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 mask
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  argument that serves a dual purpose:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa160\partightenfactor0
\ls14\ilvl0\cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	1	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Thread participation: The mask specifies which threads participate in the shuffle operation. It is a 32-bit or 64-bit integer (depending on the warp size) where the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 i
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 th bit corresponds to lane 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 i
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 . If a bit is set to 1, that thread participates; if 0, it does not.\
\ls14\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	2	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Implicit synchronization: The mask also provides automatic synchronization for all participating threads. All threads whose bits are set in the mask will be synchronized before the shuffle completes, ensuring correct data exchange even after divergent control flow.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 The default value of -1 (all bits set to 1) includes all threads in the warp.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf13 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf13 \cb14 Warning\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \cb14 When using a full mask (all bits set) in divergent code, all threads in the warp must eventually reach the shuffle instruction, even if some threads don't actively use the result. If some threads take a path that never reaches the shuffle, those threads will never arrive at the synchronization point, causing the other threads to hang indefinitely waiting for them.\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 These five primitives form the foundation for complex warp-level algorithms and serve as building blocks for higher-level collective operations.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs40 \cf2 Choosing the right shuffle primitive\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 While each shuffle primitive can technically perform any data exchange pattern, certain operations naturally fit specific use cases. Understanding these patterns helps you write more efficient and readable code.\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa160\partightenfactor0
\ls15\ilvl0\cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
If you need to share one thread's data with everyone else, reach for 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 broadcast()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  when the source is lane 0, or 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 shuffle_idx()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  for any other lane. Think of distributing a loop bound that one thread computed, or sharing a decision that a "leader" thread made.\
\ls15\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
For algorithms that process neighboring data\'97like stencil operations or convolutions\'97
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 shuffle_up()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  and 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 shuffle_down()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  are your best bet. These let you grab values from adjacent threads without the coordination overhead of shared memory. A sliding window average becomes as simple as adding your neighbors' values to your own.\
\ls15\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
When implementing tree-structured algorithms like parallel reductions, 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 shuffle_xor()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  shines. Its butterfly communication pattern naturally maps to how these algorithms exchange data. Most high-performance reduction implementations use 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 shuffle_xor()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  because it has excellent instruction scheduling properties.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 Here are some specific patterns where each primitive excels.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 shuffle_idx()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  and 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 broadcast()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  work well for:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls16\ilvl0\cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Distributing computed constants or array bounds\
\ls16\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Implementing voting mechanisms across the warp\
\ls16\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Sharing results from a designated "leader" thread\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 shuffle_up()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  and 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 shuffle_down()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  are perfect for:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls17\ilvl0\cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Stencil computations that need neighboring grid points\
\ls17\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Finite difference schemes requiring adjacent values\
\ls17\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Any sliding window algorithm (moving averages, local extrema)\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 shuffle_xor()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  excels at:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls18\ilvl0\cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Parallel reductions using butterfly patterns\
\ls18\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Any computation with power-of-2 communication strides\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 When optimizing performance, prefer shuffle operations over shared memory for register-sized data, and remember that 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 shuffle_xor()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  typically has the best instruction scheduling characteristics for reduction patterns.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs48 \cf2 Warp-level reduction operations\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

