\cf2 You'll find these primitives most useful when computing aggregates across threads that are already working closely together. Use 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 sum()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  for computing totals, averages, or accumulating values across small data segments. The 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 max()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  and 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 min()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  functions work well for finding extremes in your data or implementing voting mechanisms where threads need consensus. 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 prefix_sum()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  is particularly valuable for scan operations\'97computing running totals or building cumulative results as you process data. It's essential for algorithms that need to track "how much have we processed so far?" at each step. These operations are significantly faster than a block-level reduction that uses shared memory and 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 barrier()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  calls.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs48 \cf2 Using warp operations in practice\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 Warp operations excel in algorithms that require frequent, fine-grained data exchange between nearby threads. Common patterns include:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa160\partightenfactor0
\ls20\ilvl0\cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Sliding window operations: Use 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 shuffle_up()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  and 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 shuffle_down()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  to access neighboring lane values.\
\ls20\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Butterfly reductions: Use 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 shuffle_xor()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  for efficient tree-like reduction patterns that minimize the number of shuffle steps.\
\ls20\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Broadcasting computed values: Use 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 broadcast()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  to share a single thread's computation result (like a loop bound or pointer) across the entire warp.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 These operations are particularly valuable in algorithms where the overhead of block-level synchronization would be prohibitive, such as in inner loops of compute-intensive kernels or when processing data that naturally aligns with warp boundaries.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa800\partightenfactor0

\fs64 \cf2 Advanced synchronization mechanisms\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 Beyond the fundamental 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 barrier()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  and 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 syncwarp()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  primitives, Mojo provides additional synchronization mechanisms for specialized use cases and architecture-specific optimizations. These advanced primitives enable fine-grained control over memory ordering, asynchronous operations, and instruction scheduling. However, most of these mechanisms are available only on specific architectures. Consult the Mojo API reference documentation for the latest information on availability.\
Mechanisms currently available only on NVIDIA GPUs:\
