\fs64 \cf2 Best practices and common pitfalls\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 Now that we've covered the core synchronization primitives, let's focus on using them correctly to write reliable and portable GPU code. Understanding common pitfalls is essential for avoiding bugs that are difficult to reproduce and debug.\
This section provides guidance for writing correct, portable, and efficient GPU code:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls23\ilvl0\cf5 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\ul Writing correct synchronized code\cf2 \ulnone : Avoiding race conditions, deadlocks, and understanding when to use 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 syncwarp()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  vs. when shuffle operations handle synchronization automatically.\
\ls23\ilvl0\cf5 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\ul Choosing the right synchronization level\cf2 \ulnone : When to use warp-level operations vs. block-level synchronization.\
\ls23\ilvl0\cf5 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\ul Writing portable GPU code\cf2 \ulnone : Using Mojo's abstractions to write code that works across NVIDIA, AMD, and Apple hardware.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs48 \cf2 Writing correct synchronized code\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 Correctness should always be your first priority. The following issues are common sources of bugs in parallel programs.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs40 \cf2 Understanding and avoiding race conditions\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 As a quick reminder, a race condition occurs when multiple threads write to the same memory location without a defined order of execution, leading to a non-deterministic outcome. Here's a simple example where threads attempt to update a shared counter:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf6 \cb19 # INCORRECT: Race condition\cf17 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf17 \cb19 shared_counter\cf5 [\cf20 0\cf5 ]\cf17  \cf5 +=\cf17  my_value  \cf6 # Multiple threads modify same location\cf17 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf22 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf23 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 This leads to "lost updates" because the read-modify-write sequence isn't atomic. To prevent this, you must use synchronization primitives like 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 barrier()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  to coordinate access or use {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/std/os/atomic/Atomic/"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 \ul \ulc5 Atomic}} operations for simple updates. For example, you could use the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/std/os/atomic/Atomic/#fetch_add"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 \ul \ulc5 Atomic.fetch_add()}} method to atomically increment the counter:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf6 \cb19 # CORRECT: Atomic increment\cf17 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf17 \cb19 _ \cf5 =\cf17  Atomic\cf5 .\cf17 fetch_add\cf5 (\cf17 shared_counter\cf5 [\cf20 0\cf5 ],\cf17  my_value\cf5 )\cf17 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf22 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf23 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs40 \cf2 Avoiding deadlocks with 
\fs35 \cf10 \cb12 barrier()
\fs40 \cf2 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 A 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 barrier()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  must be encountered by all threads within a block to avoid a deadlock. Placing a 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 barrier()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  inside a conditional statement is a frequent source of bugs. If the condition causes some threads to execute the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 barrier()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  while others skip it, the threads that reach the barrier will wait indefinitely for the others to arrive, causing the kernel to hang.\
Therefore, 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 barrier()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  should be used in conditional code only if it's guaranteed that all threads in the block will evaluate the condition identically and follow the same execution path.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs40 \cf2 When to use 
\fs35 \cf10 \cb12 syncwarp()
\fs40 \cf2 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 The 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 syncwarp()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  primitive is needed when coordinating access to shared or global memory after divergent control flow within a warp. However, it is not needed before warp shuffle operations or warp reduction operations, as those operations provide their own implicit synchronization via the mask parameter.\
Use 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 syncwarp()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  when:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls24\ilvl0\cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Threads in a warp diverge and then need to synchronize before accessing shared memory\
\ls24\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
You need to ensure all threads in a warp have completed their divergent execution paths before proceeding to a shared memory operation\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 Do not use 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 syncwarp()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  before:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls25\ilvl0\cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Warp shuffle operations (
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 shuffle_down()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 shuffle_xor()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , etc.) - these synchronize automatically\
\ls25\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Warp reduction operations (
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 warp.sum()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 warp.max()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , etc.) - these also synchronize automatically\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 Here's an example where 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 syncwarp()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  is needed (for shared memory coordination):\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb19 if\cf17  thread_idx\cf5 .\cf17 x \cf5 <\cf17  \cf20 16\cf5 :\cf17 \cb1 \
\cb19     shared_data\cf5 [\cf17 thread_idx\cf5 .\cf17 x\cf5 ]\cf17  \cf5 =\cf17  compute_something\cf5 ()\cf17 \cb1 \
\cf5 \cb19 else:\cf17 \cb1 \
\cb19     shared_data\cf5 [\cf17 thread_idx\cf5 .\cf17 x\cf5 ]\cf17  \cf5 =\cf17  compute_something_else\cf5 ()\cf17 \cb1 \
\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf6 \cb19 # syncwarp() needed here because threads diverged before writing to shared memory\cf17 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf17 \cb19 syncwarp\cf5 ()\cf17 \cb1 \
\cf5 \cb19 var\cf17  result \cf5 =\cf17  shared_data\cf5 [\cf17 some_index\cf5 ]\cf17   \cf6 # Now safe to read\cf17 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf22 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf23 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 And here's an example where 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 syncwarp()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  is not needed (shuffle operations):\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb19 if\cf17  thread_idx\cf5 .\cf17 x \cf5 <\cf17  \cf20 16\cf5 :\cf17 \cb1 \
\cb19     value \cf5 =\cf17  compute_something\cf5 ()\cf17 \cb1 \
\cf5 \cb19 else:\cf17 \cb1 \
\cb19     value \cf5 =\cf17  compute_something_else\cf5 ()\cf17 \cb1 \
\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf6 \cb19 # No syncwarp() needed - shuffle_down() synchronizes automatically via its mask\cf17 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf17 \cb19 result \cf5 =\cf17  warp\cf5 .\cf17 shuffle_down\cf5 (\cf17 value\cf5 ,\cf17  \cf20 1\cf5 )\cf17 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf22 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf23 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs40 \cf2 Handling shuffle boundary conditions\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 When using 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 shuffle_up()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  and 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 shuffle_down()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , be mindful of edge cases. A thread will receive an undefined value if the source lane is out of bounds (for example, 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 current_lane - offset < 0
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 ). When implementing patterns like sliding windows, you must add logic to handle these boundary conditions correctly.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs48 \cf2 Choosing the right synchronization level\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 The core principle for designing efficient GPU algorithms is to coordinate between warps with 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 barrier()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  and shared memory, and optimize within warps with 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 gpu.primitives.warp
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  primitives.\
This hierarchical approach mirrors the GPU's architecture. Intra-warp communication is extremely fast, while cross-warp communication is more expensive.\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa160\partightenfactor0
\ls26\ilvl0\cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Use 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 gpu.primitives.warp
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  primitives for:\
\pard\tx940\tx1440\pardeftab720\li1440\fi-1440\partightenfactor0
\ls26\ilvl1\cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	
\f4 \uc0\u9702 
\f0 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
High-frequency operations inside tight loops.\
\ls26\ilvl1\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	
\f4 \uc0\u9702 
\f0 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Data exchange between neighboring threads (stencils, sliding windows).\
\ls26\ilvl1\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	
\f4 \uc0\u9702 
\f0 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Reductions or scans over small, warp-sized chunks of data.\
\ls26\ilvl1\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	
\f4 \uc0\u9702 
\f0 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Anywhere performance is latency-critical.\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa160\partightenfactor0
\ls26\ilvl0\cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Use 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 barrier()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  and 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 gpu.primitives.block
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  primitives for:\
\pard\tx940\tx1440\pardeftab720\li1440\fi-1440\partightenfactor0
\ls26\ilvl1\cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	
