\fs48 \cf2 Writing portable GPU code\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 Mojo is designed to write portable GPU code, but it's helpful to understand how.\
First, Mojo's GPU operations have automatic fallback mechanisms. For example, a 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 gpu.primitives.warp.max()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  call will automatically use specialized 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 redux
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  instructions on the newest NVIDIA hardware but will fall back to a shuffle-based implementation that works on any other GPU. You get performance where available and correctness everywhere else.\
Second, always avoid hardcoding hardware-specific values. The most common mistake is assuming a warp size of 32. Use the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/std/gpu/globals/#warp_size"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 \ul \ulc5 gpu.WARP_SIZE}} constant to ensure your code works correctly on all vendors' hardware.\
Finally, for highly-tuned kernels, you can use 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 comptime if
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  blocks to write architecture-specific code paths while keeping a single source file.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb19 from\cf17  std\cf5 .\cf17 sys \cf5 import\cf17  is_amd_gpu\cf5 ,\cf17  is_apple_gpu\cf5 ,\cf17  is_nvidia_gpu\cb1 \
\
\cf5 \cb19 def\cf17  \cf21 adaptive_algorithm\cf5 ():\cf17 \cb1 \
\cb19     \cf5 comptime\cf17  \cf5 if\cf17  is_nvidia_gpu\cf5 ():\cf17 \cb1 \
\cb19         nvidia_optimized_path\cf5 ()\cf17 \cb1 \
\cb19     \cf5 elif\cf17  is_amd_gpu\cf5 ():\cf17 \cb1 \
\cb19         amd_optimized_path\cf5 ()\cf17 \cb1 \
\cb19     \cf5 elif\cf17  is_apple_gpu\cf5 ():\cf17 \cb1 \
\cb19         apple_optimized_path\cf5 ()\cf17 \cb1 \
\cb19     \cf5 else:\cf17 \cb1 \
\cb19         \cf6 # Conservative fallback for future hardware support\cf17 \cb1 \
\cb19         portable_path\cf5 ()\cf17 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf22 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf23 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs48 \cf2 Debugging synchronization issues\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 Synchronization bugs can be tricky. Here are some strategies to find them:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa160\partightenfactor0
\ls27\ilvl0\cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Isolate the problem: Use simple, predictable data patterns (like each thread's ID) to verify your logic before using real data. Validate your parallel algorithm's output against a simple, sequential CPU version.\
\ls27\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Trace execution: Add 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 print()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  statements to trace intermediate values and understand how data flows through your warp-level shuffles or reduction trees.\uc0\u8232 \cf5 \uc0\u8232 
\fs28 \cf2 \cb16 Printing from within a kernel function is not currently supported on Apple silicon GPUs.\cb1 \uc0\u8232 \cf0 \uc0\u8232 \u8232 
\fs32 \cf5 \cb16 \uc0\u8232 \cf2 \cb1 \
\ls27\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Expose scheduling-dependent bugs: Test with different thread block sizes. A bug that appears with one configuration but not another often points to a race condition.\
\ls27\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Use dedicated tools: For complex issues, use vendor-provided GPU debugging tools (like the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://developer.nvidia.com/compute-sanitizer"}}{\fldrslt \cf5 \ul \ulc5 NVIDIA Compute Sanitizer}}) which can detect race conditions and memory access errors.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa800\partightenfactor0

\fs64 \cf2 Conclusion and key takeaways\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs48 \cf2 Summary of primitives and patterns\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 We've covered Mojo's low-level toolkit for managing concurrency and communication in GPU kernels. These primitives are the fundamental building blocks for writing correct and high-performance parallel algorithms.\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa160\partightenfactor0
\ls28\ilvl0
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
gpu.sync.barrier()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 : The essential primitive for correctness across warps. It provides a block-wide synchronization point that acts as both an execution barrier and a memory fence, primarily to coordinate access to shared memory.\
\ls28\ilvl0
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
gpu.sync.syncwarp()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 : A fine-grained primitive for managing thread divergence within a single warp. It's necessary for correctness on hardware that supports independent thread scheduling.\
\ls28\ilvl0
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
gpu.primitives.block
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  operations: High-level primitives that combine synchronization with common computational patterns (like reductions) across all threads in a block. They simplify code and are often more efficient than manual implementations.\
\ls28\ilvl0
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
gpu.primitives.warp
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  primitives: The essential toolkit for performance. By enabling direct register-to-register communication within a warp, these primitives allow for extremely fast collective operations that avoid the higher latency of shared memory.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs48 \cf2 The core mental model\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 The effective use of these primitives stems from a hierarchical approach to GPU algorithm design. Your key takeaway should be to coordinate between warps with 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 barrier()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  and shared memory, and optimize within warps with 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 gpu.primitives.warp
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  primitives.\
This principle encourages you to structure algorithms to maximize intra-warp computation and communication, which is extremely fast, and to use the more costly block-level synchronization only when necessary to combine results or manage data dependencies between warps.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

